The `test` profile expects three small files under assets/test_data/:

  - TEST01.fasta            a small Salmonella genome (or a subset of contigs
                            large enough for SeqSero2 to call a serotype)
  - TEST01_R1.fastq.gz      a small paired-end amplicon read set
  - TEST01_R2.fastq.gz

These are intentionally NOT invented here, because SeqSero2 needs biologically
real sequence to produce a meaningful serotype call — random/placeholder
sequence would give empty or misleading results and make the smoke test
worthless. To populate them, options include:

  1. Use a public Salmonella reference (e.g. an NCBI assembly) as TEST01.fasta,
     subset to the loci your primer panel targets to keep it tiny.
  2. Generate matched reads by running the cutadapt simulation step on that
     FASTA, or with a read simulator (e.g. wgsim/art_illumina) over the
     amplicon regions, then gzip them.
  3. Put real panel primers that flank those loci into primers_test.tsv so the
     simulated-amplicon path recovers sequence.

For a pure "does the DAG wire up and run" check (not a serotype-accuracy
check), any small real Salmonella FASTA plus its simulated reads is enough.